1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,517
This is MATH 239, video 26 a. The topic is matchings, covers and König's Theorem. It is chapter 8 of the graph theory notes, from page 185.

2
00:00:11,967 --> 00:00:23,066
After this video, you can use matchings and augmenting paths. You can prove that a matching is never larger than a cover. You can state König's Theorem. And you can build the sets X and Y that we use in its proof.

3
00:00:23,516 --> 00:00:28,810
Here is the graph of Figure 8.1 of the notes. It has 9 vertices and 15 edges.

4
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:39,059
A matching is a set M of edges, such that no two edges of M have a common end. The thick blue edges a h, b g and c e form a matching.

5
00:00:39,509 --> 00:00:49,120
Add the edge h g to this set. Now h is an end of two edges of the set, a h and h g. So the new set is not a matching.

6
00:00:49,570 --> 00:00:59,833
Another way to say the same condition: take the spanning subgraph with edge set M. In it, every vertex has degree 0 or 1. The labels show these degrees.

7
00:01:00,283 --> 00:01:11,057
M saturates a vertex v if v is an end of an edge of M. This matching saturates a, h, b, g, c and e. They are blue.

8
00:01:11,507 --> 00:01:15,826
The vertices d, i and f are not saturated. They are orange.

9
00:01:16,276 --> 00:01:28,767
Every graph has a matching. For example, the empty set is a matching. The question is how to find a largest matching. We call it a maximum matching. Our matching M has size 3.

10
00:01:29,217 --> 00:01:39,108
It is not maximum. Here is a matching of size 4: i a, h b, g f and c e. Now only d is not saturated.

11
00:01:39,558 --> 00:01:53,460
A perfect matching saturates every vertex. So it has size p over 2, where p is the number of vertices. Here p is 9, an odd number. So this graph has no perfect matching. A brute force check gives maximum size 4.

12
00:01:53,910 --> 00:02:11,246
Here is a problem from the notes. We have six workers, a to f, and six jobs, g to l. Each worker can do some of the jobs. Each worker gets at most one job, and each job gets at most one worker. We want to assign as many jobs as possible.

13
00:02:11,696 --> 00:02:24,606
Make a bipartite graph. Put the workers in A and the jobs in B. Join worker u to job v if u can do v. Job g goes with c and e. Job h with a and c. Job i with a, b, c, d and f.

14
00:02:25,056 --> 00:02:31,418
Then jobs j, k and l. The graph has 16 edges. This is Figure 8.2.

15
00:02:31,868 --> 00:02:45,196
An assignment is a set of edges. Each worker has at most one assigned edge, and each job has at most one. So an assignment is a matching. Here: a does h, c does i, e does k and f does l.

16
00:02:45,646 --> 00:02:55,956
So the job assignment problem is the maximum matching problem for a bipartite graph. The converse is also true: any bipartite graph gives a job assignment problem.

17
00:02:56,406 --> 00:03:14,193
Here is a practice question from Problem Set 8.2 of the notes. A domino covers two adjacent squares of a chessboard. Make a graph with one vertex for each square, and an edge for each pair of adjacent squares. Then a covering by dominoes is a perfect matching. Pause the video and try it.

18
00:03:14,643 --> 00:03:23,698
Question 5: cover all 64 squares with 32 dominoes. Put four horizontal dominoes in each row. That is 32 dominoes.

19
00:03:24,148 --> 00:03:32,136
Question 6: remove two opposite corners. Both corners have the same colour. So 30 dark squares and 32 light squares remain.

20
00:03:32,586 --> 00:03:49,229
Adjacent squares have different colours, so each domino covers one dark and one light square. 31 dominoes would need 31 dark squares. There are only 30. So no covering exists. A python check of the maximum matching gives 30, not 31.

21
00:03:49,679 --> 00:03:58,967
Fix a matching M. A path is an alternating path for M if its edges are alternately in M and not in M. The first edge can be of either kind.

22
00:03:59,417 --> 00:04:08,055
Example 1 from the notes: the path a h b g. The edge a h is in M. h b is not. b g is in M.

23
00:04:08,505 --> 00:04:17,700
Example 2: i a h g b. The edges go: not in M, in M, not in M, in M.

24
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:26,460
Example 3: i a h b g f. It has five edges: out, in, out, in, out.

25
00:04:26,910 --> 00:04:33,829
An augmenting path for M is an alternating path that joins two distinct vertices, where neither end is saturated by M.

26
00:04:34,279 --> 00:04:40,409
Example 3 is augmenting. Its ends are i and f, and M saturates neither of them.

27
00:04:40,859 --> 00:04:53,388
An end that is not saturated has no edge of M. So the first and the last edge are not in M. Thus an augmenting path has odd length. Here, 3 edges are out of M and 2 edges are in M.

28
00:04:53,838 --> 00:05:00,618
Lemma 8.1.1 says: if M has an augmenting path, then M is not a maximum matching.

29
00:05:01,068 --> 00:05:11,099
Here is the proof. Let P be an augmenting path, v 0, v 1, up to v n. The top row shows example 3.

30
00:05:11,549 --> 00:05:27,292
Edge i of the path joins v i and v i plus 1. It is in M when i is odd, and not in M when i is even. The path starts and ends with an edge not in M, so n is odd. So the path has one more edge out of M than in M.

31
00:05:27,742 --> 00:05:43,671
Now swap. Remove from M the edges of P that are in M, and add the other edges of P. Inside the path, the new edges have no common ends. The two ends of P had no edge of M before. So the result M prime is a matching, with one more edge.

32
00:05:44,121 --> 00:05:53,037
On Figure 8.1, the swap gives the matching i a, h b, g f, c e. This is the matching of size 4 that we saw before.

33
00:05:53,487 --> 00:05:59,896
A cover of a graph G is a set C of vertices, such that every edge of G has at least one end in C.

34
00:06:00,346 --> 00:06:09,727
The notes use the cover a, h, g, c, e of Figure 8.1. Add a and h first. The green edges now have an end in C.

35
00:06:10,177 --> 00:06:13,381
Add g. Four more edges become green.

36
00:06:13,831 --> 00:06:18,976
Add c and e. Now all 15 edges are green. So this set is a cover.

37
00:06:19,426 --> 00:06:33,868
Large covers are easy to find. The set of all vertices is a cover. If G is bipartite with bipartition A, B, then every edge has one end in A. So A is a cover, and B is a cover. The hard question is a minimum cover.

38
00:06:34,318 --> 00:06:49,040
Now we compare matchings and covers. Lemma 8.2.1: if M is a matching of G and C is a cover of G, then the size of M is at most the size of C. The figure shows the matching of size 4 and the cover of size 5.

39
00:06:49,490 --> 00:06:59,372
The proof. Take an edge u v of M. C is a cover, so u or v is in C. The orange dot marks one such end for each edge.

40
00:06:59,822 --> 00:07:06,509
Two different edges of M have no common end, because M is a matching. So the marked vertices of C are all different.

41
00:07:06,959 --> 00:07:14,947
So C contains at least as many vertices as M has edges. Thus the size of M is at most the size of C. Here, 4 is at most 5.

42
00:07:15,397 --> 00:07:28,028
Sometimes a cover proves that a matching is maximum. Lemma 8.2.2: if M is a matching, C is a cover, and they have the same size, then M is a maximum matching and C is a minimum cover.

43
00:07:28,478 --> 00:07:42,172
Proof. Let M prime be any matching. By Lemma 8.2.1, the size of M prime is at most the size of C, which equals the size of M. So no matching is larger than M.

44
00:07:42,622 --> 00:07:53,071
Now let C prime be any cover. By Lemma 8.2.1, its size is at least the size of M, which equals the size of C. So no cover is smaller than C.

45
00:07:53,521 --> 00:08:08,057
Here is the job graph with the matching of Figure 8.4. It has 5 edges. The green set a, c, e, i, l is a cover with 5 vertices. So by Lemma 8.2.2, this matching is maximum, and this cover is minimum.

46
00:08:08,507 --> 00:08:21,278
Look at the sizes that matchings can have in the job graph. Blue dots: there are matchings of size 0 up to 5. By Lemma 8.2.1, every matching size is at most every cover size.

47
00:08:21,728 --> 00:08:26,883
Green dots: the covers have sizes from 5 up to 12, the number of vertices.

48
00:08:27,333 --> 00:08:40,382
The largest blue size and the smallest green size meet at 5. König's Theorem says this always happens in a bipartite graph. In a bipartite graph, the maximum size of a matching is the minimum size of a cover.

49
00:08:40,832 --> 00:08:45,058
We will prove it soon. First, we see that it needs the word bipartite.

50
00:08:45,508 --> 00:09:04,160
Take the complete graph K 5. A matching has at most 2 edges, because 5 is odd. A minimum cover has 4 vertices. So there is a gap between 2 and 4. In general, K p has minimum cover p minus 1 and maximum matching p over 2, rounded down.

51
00:09:04,610 --> 00:09:16,081
Here is why a cover needs p minus 1 vertices. If a set misses two vertices u and v, then the edge u v has no end in the set. So a cover misses at most one vertex.

52
00:09:16,531 --> 00:09:39,194
Now the tool for the proof: the X Y construction. Let A, B be a bipartition of G, and M a matching. X 0 is the set of vertices of A that M does not saturate. Z is the set of vertices joined to X 0 by an alternating path. For v in Z, P of v is such a path. Then X is A intersect Z, and Y is B intersect Z.

53
00:09:39,644 --> 00:09:47,910
Take the job graph with the matching of Figure 8.2. M does not saturate b and d. So X 0 is b, d.

54
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:55,883
A path from X 0 starts with an edge not in M, because b and d have no matching edge. From b and d we reach i and l.

55
00:09:56,333 --> 00:10:01,442
The next edge must be in M. The matching edges at i and l go to c and f.

56
00:10:01,892 --> 00:10:09,369
Next, an edge not in M. From c we reach g, h, j and k. From f, we reach nothing new.

57
00:10:09,819 --> 00:10:16,303
Next, an edge in M. From h we reach a, and from k we reach e.

58
00:10:16,753 --> 00:10:33,007
No new vertex is reachable. So X is all of A, and Y is all of B. This agrees with the notes. The vertices g and j of Y are not saturated. So the alternating path b, i, c, j is augmenting, and M is not maximum.

59
00:10:33,457 --> 00:10:43,024
Here is the same graph with the matching of Figure 8.4. It has five edges: a h, b i, c j, e k and f l.

60
00:10:43,474 --> 00:10:47,375
Only d in A is not saturated. So X 0 is d.

61
00:10:47,825 --> 00:10:51,508
From d, edges not in M reach i and l.

62
00:10:51,958 --> 00:10:58,831
The matching edges at i and l go to b and f. The neighbours of b and f are only i and l, and we have them. So we stop.

63
00:10:59,281 --> 00:11:02,068
So X is d, b, f, and Y is i, l.

64
00:11:02,518 --> 00:11:11,863
To recap. A matching is a set of edges with no common ends. A cover is a set of vertices that meets every edge. An augmenting path gives a larger matching.

65
00:11:12,313 --> 00:11:25,780
A matching is never larger than a cover. If they have the same size, both are optimal. König's Theorem says that in a bipartite graph, the maximum matching and the minimum cover have the same size.

66
00:11:26,230 --> 00:11:34,729
We built the sets X and Y with alternating paths from X 0. Part 2 uses them to prove König's Theorem, and turns them into an algorithm.
